  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 PM EDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nCurrent-Tonight...More sunshine early in the period across the \ninterior with morning rainfall along the coast and adjacent \ncoastal waters providing more cloud-cover here. Highs may still \nrecover into the M-U80s across coastal counties and U80s to around\n90F in spots into the interior. Humid conditions with peak heat \nindices in the M90s to near 100F. Onshore flow will push the ECSB \ninto the interior once again. PWATs in excess of 2\" areawide with \nwarming temperatures aloft and a few subtle weak shortwave \nimpulses will traverse the area. Will see how the atmosphere \nrecovers from earlier convection, but we are still expecting SCT- \nNMRS (50-70%) shower/lightning storm chances (beginning southward)\nthru this aftn/early evening. Primary storm impacts remain \noccasional to frequent lightning strikes, gusty winds 35-50 mph \nlocally, and heavy downpours. Minor flooding remains a \npossibility, esp areas that receive repeated rounds and locations \nthat have recently seen heavy rainfall. The Weather Prediction \nCenter has recently placed south Brevard and the Treasure Coast in\na Marginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall this afternoon onward. \nThere should be a lull in convective activity mid-late evening, \nthough we will still monitor for late evening/overnight re-\ndevelopment of activity along the coast. For now, not expecting \nthe degree of rainfall totals that we observed in parts of the \nTreasure Coast this past overnight and early morning. Warm and \nhumid conditions continue, with mins in the L-M70s across the \ninterior and M-U70s at the coast. \n\nFri-Sat...Mid-level ridging centered just off of the Carolina coast \nwill drift slowly south to off of the ECFL coast. Mid-level temps \nwill remain relatively warm at -5.0C to -6.0C. At the surface, high \npressure also off the Carolina coast will get nudged further into \nthe western Atlc with assoc ridge axis remaining north of ECFL. E/SE \nwinds in the low-levels and will be enhanced each afternoon at the \nsurface with sea breeze formation and push inland. Deep layer \nmoisture remains consistent (PWATs 2.15-2.30\"). Diurnal shower \nand storm chances remain elevated (50-70%) - and will still have \nto monitor coastal locations late evening/overnight/early morning\nfor potential heavy rainfall during these periods. Primary storm \nhazards will continue to be lightning strikes, gusty winds, and \nlocally heavy downpours.\n\nMax/min temps near/slightly above normal with highs in the \nU80s-L90s and lows in the L-U70s. Peak heat indices will approach \n98-103F. Highest low temp values at the coast.\n\nSun-Wed...Mid-upper level ridging off of the central FL east coast \nwill retrograde back across the FL peninsula and emerge into the \nsouthern Gulf early next week, while on a weakening trend. This as \nan upper trough pushes into the Deep South and into north FL \nTue/Wed. At the surface, a decaying quasi-stationary front over the \nDeep South and northern Gulf will give way to an approaching weak \n\"cool\" front that will move into north FL by early Mon continuing\nto drop south into central FL thru late Mon and remaining in the \nvicinity into mid-week. Unseasonably deep moisture (PWATs > 2\") \nwill persist across the region. This will continue to promote \nhigher than normal rain chances thru the period. ISOLD strong \nstorms will be possible but warmer temps aloft may stifle this \nsomewhat. With that said, we will need to monitor for a heavy \nrainfall threat. High and low temperatures will continue to be \nslightly above normal thru Mon, but our max temps may get knocked \nback down to near to slightly below normal (esp north) behind the\nboundary.",
